6051085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049132 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049132 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049132 #edb/ref 1031 "Calculating the Cross Product Using Determinants" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049132 #edb/ref 1033 245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049132 #edb/ref 1034 0.9571568377188108 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049132 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "dd7556b7-73ec-546a-b810-4eb2042fdb48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049196 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049196 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049196 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049196 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049196 #edb/ref 1031 "Introduction to Curvature" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049196 #edb/ref 1033 1833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049196 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "e289a183-5534-5388-8ee7-3fc0d8f4ee68" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117142 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117142 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117142 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117142 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117142 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117142 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117142 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117142 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "85c76977-7b7a-54ca-bdbe-a62b6aa91c96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117143 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117143 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117143 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "93659aa9-24ed-5a6e-83fd-0c0e92811ff1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117144 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117144 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117144 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9a07c46b-72b7-5bba-90a8-3f0e9842dde6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117145 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117145 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117145 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f81ac992-3f47-5601-b282-f15c2724e4a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117146 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117146 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117146 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "39736af4-0bba-5d8a-b100-6ce561aad833" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117147 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117147 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186117147 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6b50acd0-7dfb-57a7-b7fb-7ab0a9c4b872" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119517 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12591" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119517 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle t, -2t, t^2 \\rangle$ at the point where $t = 1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119517 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f0213e01-520f-5b59-96d3-1d94a50deb82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119517 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the curvature $\\kappa (t),$ we will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t),$ $\\mathbf r'(1),$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(1)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨1,-2,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find $\\mathbf r''(t)$ and $\\mathbf r''(1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2),2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0,2⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨0,0,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-4\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(1) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(1){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{{3}^{3}}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119518 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12592" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119518 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the cross product formula for curvature, find the curvature of $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 2t, 1+t^2\\rangle$ as a function of $t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119518 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b17659af-7eb8-5922-a8b7-ab1e9dcfda08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119518 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the curvature $\\kappa (t),$ we will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩=⟨0,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\mathbf r''(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨2,\\,2t,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,2,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2t & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2t \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =4\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\| = 4,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{4}{{(2\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}})}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{2{(1+{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119519 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12608" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119519 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t^2, \\: 2t, \\: 1 \\right\\rangle$, use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature of this curve as a function of $t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119519 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0ad622cf-852f-5795-81ec-d147f050074e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119519 #edb/ref 1074 "The curvature $\\kappa (t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3} .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,2,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(2)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({t}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the second derivative $\\mathbf{r}''(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩=⟨2,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross-product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨2t,2,0⟩\\times ⟨2,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2t & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2t & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2t & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-4\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the magnitude of the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{4}{8({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}=\\frac{1}{2({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119947 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119947 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119947 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119947 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119947 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186266124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119947 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186266125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119947 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186266126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119947 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119947 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119947 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a Point" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119947 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119947 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "eddc00c0-eac2-5e79-84be-b90cc27eb054" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119948 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119948 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119948 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186266128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119948 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119948 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119948 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119948 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186367415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119948 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186368615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119948 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119948 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119948 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ca07aabe-9fdd-5f28-b39f-1971c3591f86" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119949 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119949 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186120497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119949 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119949 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119949 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119949 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119949 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119949 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186368805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119949 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a General Point" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119949 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186119519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186119949 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3b61ddc7-b781-5a25-b499-1ea6f48abf36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120035 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120035 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120035 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120035 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120035 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120035 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186120923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120035 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120035 #edb/ref 1045 "Finding Curvature Using the Cross Product" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120035 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186120918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120035 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120035 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "c5afec26-f914-51c6-81d2-b03d5e027b77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120156 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120156 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186367915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120156 #edb/ref 1069 "q-165243" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120156 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120156 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,2t⟩,$  use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature of this curve as a function of $t.$ \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=8(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j+\\,k).$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120156 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7f0173d8-1b20-5423-8c43-633f5b8b0c9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120156 #edb/ref 1074 "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}^{2}+{(2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡2t+4{cos}^{2}⁡2t+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡2t+{cos}^{2}⁡2t)+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are told that\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=8(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j+\\,k),\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =8\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡2t+{cos}^{2}⁡2t+1} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{{(2\\sqrt{2})}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{16\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120302 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186317005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120302 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34202" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120302 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120302 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $r(t)=⟨t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ at the point where $t=\\pi .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120302 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "96d2a067-654d-55aa-b5c4-b48e4bab8c2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120302 #edb/ref 1074 "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(\\pi ),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩=⟨2,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩=⟨0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ and ${r}^{″}(\\pi )$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨2,\\,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖}{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi ){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{2}{{2}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120918 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120918 #edb/ref 1061 45439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120918 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3e7c32fc-b2a5-5b01-be28-7f715f065fb1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120918 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120919 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120919 #edb/ref 1061 45443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120919 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "182c4b73-5dc9-53a4-835c-6d143dc732a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120919 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120920 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120920 #edb/ref 1061 45446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120920 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "405daa49-6e22-58fb-86e9-6e879a856c32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120920 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120921 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120921 #edb/ref 1061 45447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120921 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bf0440fe-1ba1-52ce-be45-c99625d9108a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120921 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120922 #edb/ref 1061 45450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120922 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1ca0f550-1726-5b03-9c0f-4d507624654b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120922 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186121138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120923 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186120920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120923 #edb/ref 1061 45507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120923 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "390940d4-be4b-5226-9968-df737ec5e680" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186120923 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186119949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121136 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that if the curve $\\mathbf r(s)$ is parametrized by arc length $s,$ then the curvature of $\\mathbf{r}$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\kappa(s) =  \\left\\| \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf T}{\\textrm{d}s}  \\right\\|.\n$$\n\nMoreover, if $\\mathbf r(t)$ is parametrized by a general parameter $t,$ we have the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf T(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathbf r'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|}$ is the unit tangent vector to the curve. \n\nIt can be shown that the curvature of $\\mathbf r(t)$ can also be found using the following cross product formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t)\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}\n$$\n\nWe'll derive this formula at the end of the lesson. For now, let's get some practice at applying it.\n\nFor example, suppose we would like to find the curvature of \n\\[\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\: 1, \\: t^2 \\right\\rangle\n\\]\nat the point where [math]t = 0.[/math] \n\nFirst, we calculate [math]\\mathbf r'(t),[/math] [math]\\mathbf r'(0),[/math] and [math]\\|\\mathbf r'(0)\\|\\mathbin{:}[/math]\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf r'(t) &= \\left\\langle \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}(t), \\: \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}\\left(1\\right), \\: \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}\\left(t^2\\right) \\right\\rangle \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\left \\langle 1, \\: 0, \\: 2t \\right \\rangle \\\\[7pt]\n\n\\mathbf r'(0) &= \\langle 1, 0, 0\\rangle \\\\[7pt]\n\n\\| \\mathbf r'(0) \\| & = \\sqrt{1^2 + 0^2 + 0^2} \\\\[3pt]\n&= 1\n\\end{align*}\n\nNow, we find [math]\\mathbf r''(t)[/math] and [math]\\mathbf r''(0)\\mathbin{:}[/math]\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf r''(t) & =  \\left\\langle \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}(1), \\: \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}\\left(0\\right)  \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}(2t) \\right\\rangle \\\\[5pt]\n&=\\langle 0, 0, 2 \\rangle \\\\[7pt]\n\n\\mathbf r''(0) &= \\langle 0,0,2 \\rangle\n\\end{align*}\n\nNext, we compute the cross product and its magnitude:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf r'(0) \\times \\mathbf r''(0)  & = \n\\begin{vmatrix}\n  \\mathbf i&\t\\mathbf j&\t\\mathbf k\\\\\n  1& \t\t0&\t \t0\\\\\n  0& \t\t0& \t\t2\n\\end{vmatrix}\\\\[3pt]\n&= \\begin{array}{|cc|} \n\t\t0 & 0 \\\\\n\t\t0 & 2\n\t\\end{array} \\, \\mathbf{i} - \n\\begin{array}{|cc|} \n\t\t1 & 0 \\\\\n\t\t0 & 2\n\t\\end{array} \\, \\mathbf{j} +\n\\begin{array}{|cc|} \n\t\t1 & 0 \\\\\n\t\t0 & 0\n\t\\end{array} \\, \\mathbf{k} \\\\[3pt]\n&= -2\\,\\mathbf j \\\\[7pt]\n\n\\| \\mathbf r'(0) \\times \\mathbf r''(0) \\| &= 2\n\\end{align*}\n\nTherefore, the curvature at [math]t=0[/math] is\n\n\\begin{align}\n\\kappa(0) &= \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(0) \\times \\mathbf r''(0) \\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(0)\\|^3} = \\dfrac{2}{1^3} = 2.\n\\end{align}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121136 #edb/ref 1065 7883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121136 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "699ec19c-b03f-5cc8-8096-613245aa4961" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121136 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121137 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a plane curve $C$ that is parametrized by the vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\langle x(t), \\: y(t) \\rangle.\n$$\n\nAt first glance, it looks as though the cross product curvature formula cannot be applied since cross products apply to three-dimensional vectors, whereas the given vector $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ has only two dimensions.\n\n\\[\n\\kappa(t)=\\dfrac{\\|{\\mathbf{r}^{\\prime}(t)}\\times {\\mathbf{r}^{\\prime\\prime}(t)}\\|}{\\|{\\mathbf{r}^{\\prime}(t)}\\|^3}\n\\]\n\nHowever, this curve can also be viewed as a curve in three-dimensional space that lies on the plane $z=0.$ In this case, $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ can be considered as a vector-valued function with $z$-component equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)= \\langle x(t), \\: y(t), \\: 0 \\rangle\n$$\n\nAs a result, the curvature $\\kappa$ of our plane curve $C$ can be calculated using our cross product formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t)=\\dfrac{\\|{\\mathbf{r}^{\\prime}(t)}\\times {\\mathbf{r}^{\\prime\\prime}(t)}\\|}{\\|{\\mathbf{r}^{\\prime}(t)}\\|^3}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121137 #edb/ref 1065 7884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121137 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2ae1ba4c-311d-5298-8526-146a426bda14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121137 #edb/ref 1067 "Applying the Cross Product Formula to Plane Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121138 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that if the curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ is parametrized by a general parameter $t,$ then the curvature of $\\mathbf{r}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf T(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathbf r'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|}$ is the unit tangent vector to the curve. \n\nHere, $\\mathbf r'(t)$ is a vector function and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|$ is a scalar function.\nSo, we differentiate $\\mathbf T(t)$ by using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{∂}{∂t}(T) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\frac{{r}^{'}(t)}{‖{r}^{'}(t)‖}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{'}(t)‖}\\cdot {r}^{'}(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{'}(t)‖})\\cdot {r}^{'}(t)+\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{'}(t)‖}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}({r}^{'}(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{'}(t)‖})\\cdot {r}^{'}(t)+\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{'}(t)‖}\\cdot {r}^{''}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the cross product of a vector with itself equals $\\mathbf{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t)\\times {T}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{'}(t)}{‖{r}^{'}(t)‖}\\times (\\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{'}(t)‖})\\cdot {r}^{'}(t)+\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{'}(t)‖}\\cdot {r}^{''}(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{'}(t){‖}^{2}}\\,({r}^{'}(t)\\times {r}^{''}(t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the magnitude of both sides and use the fact that $\\mathbf T$ is a unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖T(t)\\times {T}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{'}(t){‖}^{2}}\\,‖{r}^{'}(t)\\times {r}^{''}(t)‖ \\\\ ‖T(t)‖\\cdot ‖{T}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{'}(t){‖}^{2}}\\,‖{r}^{'}(t)\\times {r}^{''}(t)‖ \\\\ 1\\cdot ‖{T}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{‖{r}^{'}(t){‖}^{2}}\\,‖{r}^{'}(t)\\times {r}^{''}(t)‖ \\\\ ‖{T}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\frac{‖{r}^{'}(t)\\times {r}^{''}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{'}(t){‖}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|} = \\dfrac{\\left\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\right\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121138 #edb/ref 1065 7885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121138 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f59936dc-ef8e-53d7-be8e-244d577dd658" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186121138 #edb/ref 1067 "Deriving the Cross Product Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263233 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{t}^{2}+2}{{t}^{2}+1}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263233 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263233 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "18914e3b-4c0c-57aa-b830-1660863a7e85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263826 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263826 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263826 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5f58c6ad-9319-5d1b-9d99-ea47c8f0a8bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263908 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{